  "rationale": "**Not satisfied: a hook gated on a negated custom predicate fires even when that predicate holds.**\n\nHow the gate works today:\n\n- `dispatch_plugin_hooks` builds the active plugin set and runs only the hooks that `dispatched_hooks_for_payload` returns. No other path spawns a plugin hook. `prewarm_hook_sources` only refreshes the sources of hooks that pass the same gates (`parsed.applies` and `hook.predicates.evaluate`). It never runs a hook.\n- `dispatched_hooks_for_payload` skips the whole plugin when `parsed_plugin.applies(ctx)` is false. It then drops each hook whose `event != input.event()`, and each hook whose `matcher` is set and does not match (`InputEvent::matches_matcher`: an unanchored regex over the tool name, `*` matches everything, events without a tool always match, an invalid regex never matches). It picks the native-format match first and the symposium-format match second, and selects that hook only if `hook.predicates.evaluate(ctx)` holds. A hook in another agent's format is never selected.\n- `hook_dispatch_needs_deps` resolves the crate graph whenever a plugin-level or hook-level predicate names a concrete `depends-on(...)`, or when a chain edge or `[plugins]` entry exists. That matters because `depends-on(name)` is evaluated against `ctx.deps`. When the graph is skipped, no predicate reads it: `depends-on(*)` is always true, and `shell`, `env`, `path_exists` and `workspace-member` do not use the dependency list.\n\nTests (all pass, re-run this pass): `dispatch_skips_when_plugin_predicate_fails`, `dispatch_skips_when_hook_predicate_fails`, `dispatch_includes_when_predicates_pass`, `dispatch_respects_plugin_crate_gate` (concrete `depends-on` with and without the crate), and the integration test `matcher_filters_out_non_matching_hooks` (a Grep call fires no Bash-matched hook). An event mismatch has no direct test; it is judged from the `hook.event != input.event()` branch.\n\n**The violation: custom predicates.** The predicate reference allows a `[[predicate]]` custom predicate in any `predicates` list, including a hook's. `dispatch_plugin_hooks` builds its context with `PredicateContext::new(&dep_ids).with_used(&used)`. That context has no custom-predicate entries and no disk cache; only `sync` calls `with_custom_predicates`. Every custom predicate on the hook path therefore reaches `run_custom_predicate` with an empty map, which logs \"custom predicate not found in registry\" and returns `false`, whatever the predicate's command would answer. A plain `my_pred(x)` gate fails closed, so the hook never fires. Under `not(...)`, though, the false result becomes true. The hook fires even though its own predicate, as written and as `sync` would evaluate it, does not hold. The same happens to a plugin-level `not(custom())`.\n\nReproduced this pass with the prebuilt `target/debug/cargo-agents` and a scratch `SYMPOSIUM_HOME`. The plugin had `depends-on = [\"*\"]`, a `[[predicate]] always` whose command exits 0, and a `PreToolUse` hook with `predicates = [\"not(always())\"]`. Running `cargo agents hook claude pre-tool-use` reported \"hook neg-hook (neg): selected\", and the hook's witness script ran. With `predicates = [\"always()\"]`, the same setup reported \"skipped (hook predicates not satisfied)\" even though `always()` holds. This confirms that custom predicates always evaluate false at dispatch.\n\nEvery other case the requirement covers holds: event, matcher, plugin-level built-in predicates and hook-level built-in predicates. The determination stays false only because of the custom-predicate case.",
  "requirement": "A plugin hook MUST fire only when its event and matcher match the payload, its plugin's predicates hold, and its own predicates hold."
}
